\title{Finite PAC--Bayes Variational Bounds\\and Gaussian Perturbation Certificates}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 25, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained finite-space foundation for PAC--Bayes generalization analysis and its specialization to isotropic Gaussian perturbations. For a finite nonempty hypothesis space with strictly positive prior and posterior masses, we define expectation, relative entropy, exponential partition functions, and Gibbs tilts. We prove positivity and normalization of the tilt, Gibbs' inequality, an exact entropy identity for exponential tilting, the finite Donsker--Varadhan change-of-measure inequality, and equality at the Gibbs posterior. From a prior-weighted exponential-moment certificate we derive a risk inequality holding uniformly over all admissible posteriors. We analyze the scalar McAllester and Catoni formulas, including their gaps, monotonicity, denominator conditions, and upper-envelope properties. For Gaussian perturbations $Q=\mathcal N(w,\sigma_q^2I)$ relative to $P=\mathcal N(0,\sigma_p^2I)$, we give the explicit KL decomposition into mean-shift and variance-mismatch costs, prove its nonnegativity and equal-variance reduction, and insert it into a computable McAllester certificate. Finally, we establish vanishing fixed-complexity penalties and an explicit equal-variance rate. The results isolate the deterministic variational core from sample-level concentration and clarify which additional ingredients are needed for classifier tightness.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

PAC--Bayes theory studies randomized predictors by comparing a data-dependent posterior distribution $Q$ with a reference prior distribution $P$. Its characteristic complexity is the Kullback--Leibler divergence $\KL(Q\|P)$: selecting a posterior far from the prior incurs an information cost.

The argument has two layers. A probabilistic layer proves, under random sampling, that a suitable exponential moment is controlled with high probability. A variational layer converts that prior-weighted moment into a posterior-uniform risk statement. We isolate the second layer on a finite hypothesis space. Its central inequality is
\[
  \E_Q[a]\leq \KL(Q\|P)+\log \E_P[e^a].
\]
The exponentially tilted distribution attains equality, showing that Gibbs weighting is the exact optimizer of expected score minus information cost.

We then study McAllester's square-root expression and Catoni's exponential expression. Their algebraic properties are distinguished from the sample-level premises required to make them true-risk guarantees. Finally, isotropic Gaussian perturbations produce an explicit complexity with a mean-shift term and a variance-mismatch term. This yields a numerical pipeline relevant to randomized neural-network predictors.

The scope is precise. Strict positivity is assumed in the finite theory. Sample-level concentration is left as an explicit premise. Vanishing upper penalties are established, but asymptotic tightness for classifiers is not claimed without a specified data model and matching lower bound.

\section{Finite probability and relative entropy}

Let $H$ be a finite nonempty set. A strictly positive probability mass function is a map $q:H\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $q(h)>0$ and $\sum_hq(h)=1$.

\begin{definition}[Expectation and relative entropy]
For $x:H\to\mathbb R$, define
\[
 \E_q[x]=\sum_{h\in H}q(h)x(h).
\]
For strictly positive probability mass functions $q,p$, define
\[
 \KL(q\|p)=\sum_{h\in H}q(h)\log\frac{q(h)}{p(h)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partition function and Gibbs tilt]
For a prior $p$ and score $a:H\to\mathbb R$, let
\[
 Z_p(a)=\sum_{h\in H}p(h)e^{a(h)},\qquad
 p_a(h)=\frac{p(h)e^{a(h)}}{Z_p(a)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Positive partition function]
If $p(h)>0$ for every $h$, then $Z_p(a)>0$ for every real score $a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every term $p(h)e^{a(h)}$ is strictly positive, and the nonempty finite sum is therefore strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Normalization of the Gibbs tilt]
Under the preceding assumptions, $p_a(h)>0$ for every $h$ and $\sum_hp_a(h)=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The numerator and denominator are positive. Summation gives $Z_p(a)/Z_p(a)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gibbs' inequality]
If $q$ and $p$ are strictly positive probability mass functions on $H$, then
\[
 \KL(q\|p)\geq0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply $\log x\leq x-1$ to $x=p(h)/q(h)>0$. Negating and multiplying by $q(h)>0$ gives
\[
 q(h)\log\frac{q(h)}{p(h)}\geq q(h)-p(h).
\]
Summation yields $\KL(q\|p)\geq\sum_hq(h)-\sum_hp(h)=0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Exponential tilting and change of measure}

\begin{theorem}[Exact tilt identity]
Let $q,p$ be strictly positive probability mass functions and $a:H\to\mathbb R$. Then
\[
 \KL(q\|p_a)=\KL(q\|p)-\E_q[a]+\log Z_p(a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The definition of $p_a$ gives
\[
 \log\frac{q(h)}{p_a(h)}
 =\log\frac{q(h)}{p(h)}-a(h)+\log Z_p(a).
\]
Multiply by $q(h)$ and sum. The final constant is multiplied by $\sum_hq(h)=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite change of measure]
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
 \E_q[a]\leq \KL(q\|p)+\log Z_p(a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The tilt is a strictly positive probability mass function. Apply Gibbs' inequality to $\KL(q\|p_a)$, substitute the exact tilt identity, and rearrange.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gibbs posterior attains equality]
For strictly positive $p$ and arbitrary $a$,
\[
 \E_{p_a}[a]-\KL(p_a\|p)=\log Z_p(a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $q=p_a$ in the exact tilt identity and use $\KL(p_a\|p_a)=0$.
\end{proof}

Thus the log partition function is the variational optimum
\[
 \log Z_p(a)=\max_q\{\E_q[a]-\KL(q\|p)\}
\]
over strictly positive probability mass functions. Expected score and information cost form an exact free-energy duality.

\section{From a moment certificate to posterior-uniform risk}

Let $\Rhat,R:H\to\mathbb R$ be empirical and true risks. Fix $\eta>0$ and $\delta>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Moment-to-risk conversion]
Suppose $p$ is a strictly positive prior and
\[
 \sum_{h\in H}p(h)
 \exp\!\left(\eta(R(h)-\Rhat(h))\right)\leq\frac1\delta.
\]
Then every strictly positive posterior $q$ satisfies
\[
 \E_q[R]\leq \E_q[\Rhat]
 +\frac{\KL(q\|p)+\log(1/\delta)}{\eta}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply finite change of measure to $a(h)=\eta(R(h)-\Rhat(h))$. Linearity gives
\[
 \E_q[a]=\eta(\E_q[R]-\E_q[\Rhat]).
\]
The partition function is positive, so monotonicity of the logarithm and the hypothesis give $\log Z_p(a)\leq\log(1/\delta)$. Substitute and divide by $\eta>0$.
\end{proof}

The theorem is deterministic conditional on the moment certificate. A statistical theorem must separately prove that this certificate holds with the desired sampling probability, commonly using concentration and Markov's inequality.

\section{The McAllester expression}

\begin{definition}[McAllester scalar expression]
For empirical risk $\widehat r$, complexity $k$, sample size $n$, and confidence parameter $\delta$, set
\[
 B_M(\widehat r,k,n,\delta)=\widehat r+
 \sqrt{\frac{k+\log(2\sqrt n/\delta)}{2(n-1)}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Exact nonnegative gap]
\[
 B_M(\widehat r,k,n,\delta)-\widehat r
 =\sqrt{\frac{k+\log(2\sqrt n/\delta)}{2(n-1)}}\geq0.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Cancel $\widehat r$ and use nonnegativity of the real square root.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in complexity]
If $n>1$ and $k_1\leq k_2$, then
\[
 B_M(\widehat r,k_1,n,\delta)\leq B_M(\widehat r,k_2,n,\delta).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The denominator is positive; division and the square root preserve order.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Subadditive square-root penalty]
For $x,y\geq0$, $\sqrt{x+y}\leq\sqrt x+\sqrt y$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both sides are nonnegative, and $(\sqrt x+\sqrt y)^2=x+y+2\sqrt{xy}\geq x+y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[McAllester certificate rule]
If a sample-level argument establishes
\[
 R(q)\leq\Rhat(q)+
 \sqrt{\frac{\KL(q\|p)+\log(2\sqrt n/\delta)}{2(n-1)}},
\]
then $R(q)\leq B_M(\Rhat(q),\KL(q\|p),n,\delta)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is direct substitution into the definition. The premise contains the substantive probabilistic content.
\end{proof}

\section{The Catoni expression}

\begin{definition}[Catoni scalar expression]
For inverse temperature $\lambda>0$, define
\[
 B_C(\widehat r,k,n,\delta,\lambda)=
 \frac{1-\exp\!\left(-\lambda\widehat r-
 (k+\log(1/\delta))/n\right)}{1-e^{-\lambda}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Positive denominator]
If $\lambda>0$, then $1-e^{-\lambda}>0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $-\lambda<0$, strict monotonicity of the exponential gives $e^{-\lambda}<e^0=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in empirical risk]
If $\lambda>0$ and $\widehat r_1\leq\widehat r_2$, then
\[
 B_C(\widehat r_1,k,n,\delta,\lambda)
 \leq B_C(\widehat r_2,k,n,\delta,\lambda).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Increasing $\widehat r$ decreases the exponent and hence its exponential. One minus that exponential increases, and the denominator is positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in complexity]
If $\lambda>0$, $n>0$, and $k_1\leq k_2$, then
\[
 B_C(\widehat r,k_1,n,\delta,\lambda)
 \leq B_C(\widehat r,k_2,n,\delta,\lambda).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Increasing $k$ decreases the exponent by a nonnegative amount. Apply exponential monotonicity and divide by the positive denominator.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Scalar upper envelope]
For $\lambda>0$,
\[
 B_C(\widehat r,k,n,\delta,\lambda)\leq\frac1{1-e^{-\lambda}}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The exponential is nonnegative, so the numerator is at most one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Catoni certificate rule]
If a Catoni moment argument establishes
\[
 R(q)\leq
 \frac{1-\exp\!\left(-\lambda\Rhat(q)-
 (\KL(q\|p)+\log(1/\delta))/n\right)}{1-e^{-\lambda}},
\]
then $R(q)\leq B_C(\Rhat(q),\KL(q\|p),n,\delta,\lambda)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the definition of $B_C$.
\end{proof}

If valid McAllester and Catoni premises are both available, true risk lies below both formulas. No unconditional ordering is asserted. In particular, arbitrary scalar inputs do not force the Catoni expression to exceed empirical risk.

\section{Gaussian perturbation posteriors}

Consider
\[
 Q=\mathcal N(w,\sigma_q^2I),\qquad
 P=\mathcal N(0,\sigma_p^2I)
\]
on $\mathbb R^d$, with $\sigma_q,\sigma_p>0$ and $r=\lVert w\rVert$.

\begin{definition}[Isotropic Gaussian complexity]
\[
 K_G(d,r,\sigma_q,\sigma_p)=
 \frac{r^2}{2\sigma_p^2}+
 \frac d2\left(\frac{\sigma_q^2}{\sigma_p^2}-1-
 \log\frac{\sigma_q^2}{\sigma_p^2}\right).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Variance-mismatch inequality]
For $x>0$, $x-1-\log x\geq0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Rearrange $\log x\leq x-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegative Gaussian complexity]
If $\sigma_q,\sigma_p>0$, then $K_G(d,r,\sigma_q,\sigma_p)\geq0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The shift term is a square divided by a positive quantity. Put $x=\sigma_q^2/\sigma_p^2>0$ in the variance-mismatch inequality and multiply by $d/2\geq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal-variance reduction]
For $\sigma>0$,
\[
 K_G(d,r,\sigma,\sigma)=\frac{r^2}{2\sigma^2}.
\]
Consequently $K_G(d,0,\sigma,\sigma)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The variance ratio is one and $1-1-\log1=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in squared norm]
For fixed $d,\sigma_q$ and $\sigma_p>0$, if $r_1^2\leq r_2^2$, then
\[
 K_G(d,r_1,\sigma_q,\sigma_p)
 \leq K_G(d,r_2,\sigma_q,\sigma_p).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The mismatch terms agree, and division by $2\sigma_p^2>0$ preserves the ordering.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit Gaussian McAllester certificate]
For $n>1$, define
\[
 C_G=\widehat r+
 \sqrt{\frac{K_G(d,r,\sigma_q,\sigma_p)+
 \log(2\sqrt n/\delta)}{2(n-1)}}.
\]
Then $C_G$ is exactly the McAllester expression after substitution of the isotropic Gaussian KL complexity, and $\widehat r\leq C_G$. Whenever the corresponding sample-level McAllester premise holds for the Gaussian randomized predictor, its true risk is at most $C_G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $K_G$ into the definition of $B_M$. The square-root penalty is nonnegative. The final assertion applies the McAllester certificate rule.
\end{proof}

The first part of $K_G$ measures displacement of the posterior mean. The second measures variance mismatch. In high dimension the latter can dominate, while equal variances eliminate it.

\section{Algorithms and numerical use}

\subsection{Finite variational audit}
Given arrays $(p_h,q_h,a_h)$, compute
\[
 Z=\sum_hp_he^{a_h},\qquad p_a(h)=p_he^{a_h}/Z,
\]
then compare $L=\sum_hq_ha_h$ with
\[
 U=\sum_hq_h\log(q_h/p_h)+\log Z.
\]
The change-of-measure theorem gives $L\leq U$, and the exact identity gives $U-L=\KL(q\|p_a)$. The procedure costs $O(|H|)$ time and $O(|H|)$ storage if the tilt is retained.

\subsection{Gaussian certificate computation}
Validate $d\geq0$, $n>1$, $0<\delta<1$, and positive scales. Compute $x=\sigma_q^2/\sigma_p^2$, then
\[
 k=\frac{r^2}{2\sigma_p^2}+\frac d2(x-1-\log x),
\]
and return $B_M(\widehat r,k,n,\delta)$. This takes constant scalar time once $r$ is known, or $O(d)$ time when the norm is computed from a $d$-component vector.

\subsection{Catoni temperature search}
For a predetermined finite grid $\Lambda\subset(0,\infty)$, evaluate $B_C$ at every $\lambda\in\Lambda$ and return the least finite value. This costs $O(|\Lambda|)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. If data select the temperature, rigorous use must account for the search, for example through advance grid choice and confidence allocation.

For neural networks, parameters may be flattened into $w$, Gaussian perturbations sampled to estimate posterior-averaged empirical loss, and $K_G$ evaluated analytically. Relating randomized loss back to the unperturbed network requires an additional stability, margin, or derandomization theorem.

\section{Asymptotic behavior}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing fixed square-root complexity]
For fixed $C\geq0$,
\[
 \sqrt{\frac{C}{n+1}}\longrightarrow0
 \qquad(n\to\infty).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $n+1\to\infty$, the quotient tends to zero. Continuity of the square root at zero completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing Gaussian McAllester argument]
Fix $d,r$, positive $\sigma_q,\sigma_p$, and $\delta>0$. Then
\[
 \frac{K_G(d,r,\sigma_q,\sigma_p)+\log(2\sqrt n/\delta)}{2(n-1)}
 \longrightarrow0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Gaussian complexity is constant in $n$, while
\[
 \log(2\sqrt n/\delta)=\log2+\tfrac12\log n-\log\delta.
\]
Constants divided by $n-1$ vanish, and $\log n/(n-1)\to0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under the same fixed-parameter assumptions, the Gaussian McAllester square-root penalty converges to zero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply continuity of the square root at zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equal-variance rate]
If $\sigma>0$ and $r^2\leq C$, then for every $n>1$,
\[
 \frac{K_G(d,r,\sigma,\sigma)}{n}
 =\frac{r^2}{2\sigma^2n}
 \leq\frac{C}{2\sigma^2n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use the equal-variance identity and divide $r^2\leq C$ by $2\sigma^2n>0$.
\end{proof}

These statements are consistency ingredients for upper certificates. They do not prove asymptotic tightness for linear classifiers; that requires a specified distribution, loss, assumptions, and matching lower bound.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The finite theory rests on a short exact chain: positive masses yield a normalized tilt; $\log x\leq x-1$ yields nonnegative relative entropy; the exact tilt identity yields change of measure; and a moment certificate yields posterior-uniform risk control. The strict-positivity assumption can be relaxed by using support conditions and extended relative entropy, or by passing to Radon--Nikodym derivatives on measurable spaces.

A complete sample-level McAllester theorem should introduce i.i.d. bounded losses, prove the needed exponential moment through a Hoeffding-type estimate, and use Markov's inequality to construct one high-probability event uniform over posteriors. A sample-level Catoni theorem should derive the nonlinear transform from the Bernoulli moment inequality and account rigorously for temperature optimization.

For Gaussian perturbations, a measure-level derivation from densities would connect the closed formula directly to Gaussian laws. Neural-network applications further require perturbation or margin bounds that relate randomized predictors to deterministic networks. Finally, linear-classifier tightness requires matching lower and upper rates under explicit margin or noise assumptions. The present vanishing-penalty theorem supplies only the upper consistency component.

\section{Conclusion}

Exponential tilting is the exact optimizer that balances posterior score against relative entropy. A prior exponential-moment bound therefore becomes a statement uniform over admissible posteriors. McAllester and Catoni formulas express this principle in square-root and exponential forms, while isotropic Gaussian perturbations make the information cost explicit as mean-shift plus variance mismatch. The resulting quantities are computable and their fixed-parameter penalties vanish with increasing sample size. Sample-level concentration, support-general measure theory, deterministic-network control, and matching lower bounds are the natural next stages of the theory.

